hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied in taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  within the self-consciousness section of  Hegel's phenomenology one of the key  developments that he narrates for us  that tells us about the very nature of  our own self-consciousness is what we  can call a doubling and ambiguity  there's a play on words there going on  of consciousness and he's doing this  primarily in paragraphs 178 to 184 this  is prior to the struggle to the death  and the ensuing dialectic of Master and  slave or lordship and bondage that  people tend to focus on but this is a  very important aspect of  self-consciousness that he's revealing  to us the relation to the other is at  the same time a being related to the  other because by the very fact of being  related to another self-consciousness  there's already a mirroring or duality  going on if I am relating myself to that  person out there and and I'm doing it as  sort of another me or somebody similar  in structure to myself as another human  being  they are similarly doing the same thing  in relation to me as well at least at  this stage of the dialectic as Hegel is  narrating so he tells us that  self-consciousness exists in and for  itself only through by existing as such  for an other so this means that  self-consciousness cannot be what it is  without existing in relation to another  another that also relates itself to that  self-consciousness so let's talk very  briefly about this in and for itself  language Hegel uses this quite a bit  he'll distinguish between being in  itself which is the being of an object  you might say a kind of inert being  sometimes it can be dynamic then being  for self which is what we typically do  associate with self-consciousness I  exist  for myself by making my own self an  object for my own thought or my own  action by asking myself what should i do  today I'm already treating myself as  being for self and the goal for Hegel  over and over again is to have these  fuse together in some sort of dynamic  equilibrium or harmony or however you  want to put it Hegel would put it in  terms of a greater unity that that's  thereby attained but this unity cannot  be this is one of the key ideas cannot  be attained except by a kind of duality  a duality of self and other so you can't  have being in itself or being for itself  or both of them without existing as such  for an other it's in a certain sense if  you do have being in itself and being  for itself it's never quite stable you  can't be certain to use Hegel's  favourite term here you can't be certain  of your being in and for self unless it  exists in terms of another and this  brings us to this key notion of  recognition on air kenan in Hegel's  german which is getting fleshed out here  a bit the structure the nature of human  recognition of each other and its  reciprocity so you notice that I have  this nice schema here this will be a bit  useful Hegel tells us as we go through  this that self-consciousness is faced by  another self-consciousness and he says  it has come out of itself and this  introduces to us the when Hegel is  calling the first ambiguity now this is  something I think is worth talking about  the the Greek word that we get ambiguity  from this um be here means both or two  right so when we say something is  ambiguous we don't mean that it's  unclear we mean that it could be  interpreted multiple ways and any  unclarity that comes from that is  precisely because of that ambiguity the  term that a lot of people misuse because  they mistake ambiguity for unclarity or  fuzziness or thing  like that strictly speaking it means  that there's multiple ways to interpret  the situation to make sense other things  the German word really captures as well  doleful the word we get you know is a  cognitive double from doable then then  means meaning or sense so when we say  that a term has more than one sense and  each of those senses is legitimate we  have a double chin or perhaps a you know  a triple or quadruple or whatever sense  but this adequately captures what we're  talking about here because this is the  self-consciousness is trying to figure  out how it exists in relation to the  other self-consciousness so it looks at  the other self-consciousness and Hegel  says in a sense it loses itself because  it goes out of itself and then finds an  other a different person who isn't it  and who is independent of it who could  withhold that recognition who could say  I don't care about you you don't matter  or who are you or whatever other sort of  comportment we can think of on the other  hand it also recognizes the other  self-consciousness as other  self-consciousness precisely by seeing  its own self in that other  self-consciousness so what I put here is  the self of self-consciousness exist  outside of itself in another and there's  desire for that on the part of that that  first self-consciousness that we are  considering in this process so he says  if lost itself or it finds itself as an  other being in doing so it is also  superseded the other this is another key  Hegelian term Alf Haven which we might  think of integrating into a greater  whole or transcending sometimes as  nullifying it's trying to negotiate its  relationship to the other by figuring  out how is that other like itself how  does it find itself in the other in the  eyes  of the other or in the action of the  other or in the desire of the other so  Hegel then starts talking about  ambiguity here and he says you know we  what we have is a first ambiguity right  and we transcend that or supersede that  and that then this super session of  other nough speak um a second ambiguity  what does that mean Hegel is telling us  that there's multiple ways in which this  can be interpreted you could think of  this as a situation in which multiple  resolutions are possible or are  suggesting themselves to the  self-consciousness that is now  confronting engaging hoping for  something from its other who it is  encountering and he goes on and tells us  that it has to supersede the other  independent being in order to become  certain of itself as the essential being  this is integral to Hegel's treatment of  self-consciousness in his view we are  constantly engaging with the other in  such a way as to try to remove the  independence of the other and to have  them fit into the ways in which we see  things the way in which we want things  this is a constant struggle for people  in relationships ranging from romantic  relationships to colleagues at work to  people trying to talk across cultural or  ideological divides this need to to make  the other fit into one's own categories  stems Hegel thinks from a desire to  attain certainty of oneself through the  other and and the other sort of has to  be fixed in place in order to provide  that but there's a paradox here and in  this case it's that well what's this  other doing is the other just taking its  cues entirely from from me and what my  desires are and what I'm telling it or  is this other since it is another me  perhaps more independent than I thought  it was  it also exhibits the exact same agency  the exact same desire the same loss the  same seeing the 