gnize  is that both of these are on each side  so there's a doubling of  self-consciousness as well as an  ambiguity that is taking place he tells  us the other is equally independent and  self-contained there is nothing in it of  which it is not itself the origin this  is a very important thought many people  in their relationship as  self-consciousness is trying to attain  recognition from other self  consciousnesses do not understand that  what they're seeking is inherently  paradoxical what they want is  recognition from a nother and other  self-consciousness who must be  independent in order to actually grant  recognition if they want to be  acknowledged as self-consciousness  existing in and for itself they need to  get that from another being that is  likewise in and for itself and this  applies again to both sides so he goes  on and he tells us the first does not  have the object before it merely as it  exists primarily for desire but  something that has an independent  existence of its own which it cannot  just utilize for its own purposes if I  reduce the other merely to an object of  my desire I lose the other in the  process and do not attain what my desire  is so he says thus the movement is  simply the double movement of the two  self consciousnesses here's where a  mirroring takes place each sees the  other do the same as it does each does  itself what it demands of the other and  therefore also does what it does only  insofar as the other does the same  action by one side only would be useless  because what is to happen can only be  brought about by both that's a very  important consideration there we can't  get what we actually need  in terms of reciprocity by simply  demanding it or by attempting to provoke  it on the one hand exercising our agency  or by receptively trying to seduce it  out of the other there has to be a  mutual recognition taking place  indeterminate circumstances that  acknowledges that both are the other to  the other that acknowledge that both are  motivated by desire that both are  looking for recognition from somebody  who is an equal whether or not that is  possible depends and this is the last  thing that I think we should talk about  here depends for Hegel on what  historical stage of consciousness we  have attained we're going to see that in  the master-slave dialectic where you  don't have a high level of culture to  provide sort of an apparatus to to  mediate recognition the two conte the  two consciousnesses the two self  consciousnesses are going to have to  engage in a struggle to the death one is  going to have to subjugate the other  that is not where Hegel wants to  ultimately go and he thinks that human  civilization has progressed beyond that  not not fully but to the point where at  least some are able to attain  recognition without resorting to that  you
 

